1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,633
Chapter six. Color and giant screen.

2
00:00:03,983 --> 00:00:09,833
Color arrived slowly. Méliès was already painting some of his films by hand, frame by frame.

3
00:00:10,283 --> 00:00:18,811
The real breakthrough came with three colors: red, green, and blue. Mixing those three lights creates almost any color.

4
00:00:19,011 --> 00:00:26,960
In the 1930s, a camera filmed each color onto a separate strip of film, then combined them. It was called Technicolor.

5
00:00:27,160 --> 00:00:34,840
In 1939, The Wizard of Oz began in sepia. When a door opened, the world exploded into color.

6
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:41,828
In the 1950s, television stole audiences away from movie theaters. The answer was to do what a TV set couldn't.

7
00:00:42,028 --> 00:00:47,029
Huge, wider screens, and even 3D movies with special glasses.

8
00:00:47,229 --> 00:00:55,936
And in the 1970s, with Jaws and Star Wars, the summer blockbuster was born. Lines wrapped all the way around the block.

9
00:00:56,836 --> 00:01:01,436
Every time a rival screen appeared, cinema got bigger.
